1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,264
If you were a father in nineteen

2
00:00:02,264 --> 00:00:05,236
sixty, you were probably in your thirties.

3
00:00:05,416 --> 00:00:07,986
You had served in the Second World

4
00:00:07,986 --> 00:00:08,968
War or Korea.

5
00:00:09,148 --> 00:00:10,458
You had come home,

6
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,204
married your sweetheart,

7
00:00:12,204 --> 00:00:14,459
found a steady job and bought a

8
00:00:14,459 --> 00:00:14,895
house.

9
00:00:15,075 --> 00:00:16,385
Now you had two,

10
00:00:16,385 --> 00:00:17,941
three or four kids,

11
00:00:17,941 --> 00:00:20,069
a mortgage, a car payment,

12
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:23,016
and a whole family depending on you.

13
00:00:23,196 --> 00:00:25,653
And you probably never once complained about

14
00:00:25,653 --> 00:00:25,820
it.

15
00:00:26,171 --> 00:00:29,854
Fathers of nineteen sixty are often remembered

16
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:31,616
as quiet, serious men.

17
00:00:31,796 --> 00:00:33,976
Men in hats and overcoats,

18
00:00:33,976 --> 00:00:37,078
carrying a briefcase or a lunch pail.

19
00:00:37,258 --> 00:00:38,930
Men who didn't say much,

20
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:41,368
but who showed up every single day.

21
00:00:41,548 --> 00:00:44,516
Many of us didn't really understand our

22
00:00:44,516 --> 00:00:47,713
fathers until we became parents ourselves.

23
00:00:48,063 --> 00:00:50,194
So let's spend a week as a

24
00:00:50,194 --> 00:00:51,998
dad in nineteen sixty.

25
00:00:52,178 --> 00:00:54,606
From Monday morning at work to Sunday

26
00:00:54,606 --> 00:00:56,311
afternoon in the backyard.

27
00:00:56,491 --> 00:00:58,439
If your father was one of these

28
00:00:58,439 --> 00:01:00,325
men, I think you'll see him in

29
00:01:00,325 --> 00:01:01,519
almost every scene.

30
00:01:01,869 --> 00:01:04,121
The alarm clock rang at six.

31
00:01:04,301 --> 00:01:06,542
A wind-up clock with two bells on

32
00:01:06,542 --> 00:01:08,851
top, loud enough to wake the whole

33
00:01:08,851 --> 00:01:09,258
house.

34
00:01:09,438 --> 00:01:12,253
Dad shaved at the bathroom sink with

35
00:01:12,253 --> 00:01:14,520
a safety razor and a brush of

36
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:15,615
shaving cream.

37
00:01:15,795 --> 00:01:18,806
A splash of Old Spice or Aqua

38
00:01:18,806 --> 00:01:19,429
Velva.

39
00:01:19,609 --> 00:01:21,385
Brylcreem in his hair.

40
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:23,958
Then he put on a white shirt,

41
00:01:23,958 --> 00:01:25,361
a tie and a suit,

42
00:01:25,361 --> 00:01:28,415
or work clothes and steel-toed boots.

43
00:01:28,765 --> 00:01:30,449
Breakfast was eggs,

44
00:01:30,449 --> 00:01:33,109
bacon, toast and black coffee,

45
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:34,616
made by his wife.

46
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:37,188
He read the morning newspaper at the

47
00:01:37,188 --> 00:01:39,579
kitchen table, folded into quarters.

48
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:41,269
He smoked a cigarette.

49
00:01:41,449 --> 00:01:43,784
The kids came down in their pajamas,

50
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:45,471
and he gave each of them a

51
00:01:45,471 --> 00:01:47,288
quick pat on the head on his

52
00:01:47,288 --> 00:01:48,391
way out the door.

53
00:01:48,742 --> 00:01:49,856
Then off to work.

54
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:50,991
In the suburbs,

55
00:01:50,991 --> 00:01:53,219
he might drive to the train station

56
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:55,511
and ride the commuter train into the

57
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:55,829
city.

58
00:01:56,009 --> 00:01:57,329
In a factory town,

59
00:01:57,329 --> 00:01:59,968
he walked or carpooled to the plant.

60
00:02:00,148 --> 00:02:00,957
On a farm,

61
00:02:00,957 --> 00:02:04,189
he'd already been working for two hours.

62
00:02:04,369 --> 00:02:07,167
Either way, he usually carried his lunch.

63
00:02:07,347 --> 00:02:09,327
A sandwich in wax paper,

64
00:02:09,327 --> 00:02:12,049
an apple and a thermos of coffee.

65
00:02:12,399 --> 00:02:13,599
In nineteen sixty,

66
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:15,666
a man often worked for the same

67
00:02:15,666 --> 00:02:17,600
company for his whole career.

68
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,235
Thirty or forty years at the same

69
00:02:20,235 --> 00:02:22,764
plant, the same office or the same

70
00:02:22,764 --> 00:02:23,434
railroad.

71
00:02:23,614 --> 00:02:25,692
Loyalty went both ways.

72
00:02:25,872 --> 00:02:28,248
The company promised steady work and a

73
00:02:28,248 --> 00:02:30,562
pension, and the man promised to show

74
00:02:30,562 --> 00:02:31,375
up every day.

75
00:02:31,726 --> 00:02:34,375
Office men worked at metal desks,

76
00:02:34,375 --> 00:02:36,463
surrounded by typewriters,

77
00:02:36,463 --> 00:02:39,353
adding machines and filing cabinets.

78
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:42,563
Secretaries took dictation in shorthand.

79
00:02:42,743 --> 00:02:45,158
Meetings were full of cigarette smoke.

80
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:48,854
A three-martini lunch was not unusual for

81
00:02:48,854 --> 00:02:49,797
executives.

82
00:02:49,977 --> 00:02:51,554
And the men all wore the same

83
00:02:51,554 --> 00:02:51,881
thing.

84
00:02:52,061 --> 00:02:53,843
A gray flannel suit,

85
00:02:53,843 --> 00:02:56,159
a narrow tie and a fedora.

86
00:02:56,509 --> 00:02:59,368
Factory men worked eight-hour shifts,

87
00:02:59,368 --> 00:03:01,919
often with overtime on Saturdays.

88
00:03:02,099 --> 00:03:03,705
The work was loud,

89
00:03:03,705 --> 00:03:05,222
hot and physical.

90
00:03:05,402 --> 00:03:07,971
Steel mills, auto plants,

91
00:03:07,971 --> 00:03:10,951
rubber factories, coal mines.

92
00:03:11,131 --> 00:03:13,037
A union job paid well,

93
00:03:13,037 --> 00:03:16,068
often enough to support a family of

94
00:03:16,068 --> 00:03:16,414
six.

95
00:03:16,594 --> 00:03:19,183
But many men came home exhausted,

96
00:03:19,183 --> 00:03:21,772
with sore backs and ringing ears.

97
00:03:22,122 --> 00:03:24,499
Many men worked a second job,

98
00:03:24,499 --> 00:03:24,827
too.

99
00:03:25,007 --> 00:03:26,784
A few nights a week at a

100
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:29,746
gas station, driving a delivery truck on

101
00:03:29,746 --> 00:03:33,227
weekends, or doing carpentry for the neighbors.

102
00:03:33,407 --> 00:03:35,896
Farmers often worked at a factory in

103
00:03:35,896 --> 00:03:36,657
the winter.

104
00:03:36,837 --> 00:03:39,345
Teachers painted houses in the summer.

105
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:41,911
The extra money paid for braces,

106
00:03:41,911 --> 00:03:44,671
piano lessons or a family vacation at

107
00:03:44,671 --> 00:03:45,342
the lake.

108
00:03:45,692 --> 00:03:47,038
What did dad earn?

109
00:03:47,218 --> 00:03:48,559
In nineteen sixty,

110
00:03:48,559 --> 00:03:51,240
a man working full time earned about

111
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:53,772
fifty-four hundred dollars a year,

112
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:54,591
on average.

113
00:03:54,771 --> 00:03:56,763
That was enough for a house,

114
00:03:56,763 --> 00:03:57,617
a car, food,

115
00:03:57,617 --> 00:04:00,037
clothes and a little left over for

116
00:04:00,037 --> 00:04:00,819
a vacation.

117
00:04:00,999 --> 00:04:02,175
In most families,

118
00:04:02,175 --> 00:04:04,249
his paycheck was the only one,

119
00:04:04,249 --> 00:04:05,701
and everyone knew it.

120
00:04:06,052 --> 00:04:08,237
At five-thirty or six,

121
00:04:08,237 --> 00:04:09,627
Dad came home.

122
00:04:09,807 --> 00:04:10,995
In many houses,

123
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:12,420
this was a ritual.

124
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:14,515
The kids ran to the door.

125
00:04:14,695 --> 00:04:16,692
Mom had dinner almost ready.

126
00:04:16,872 --> 00:04:19,112
Dad hung up his hat and coat,

127
00:04:19,112 --> 00:04:20,425
loosened his tie,

128
00:04:20,425 --> 00:04:22,433
and sat down in his chair.

129
00:04:22,613 --> 00:04:24,457
Maybe he had a cocktail,

130
00:04:24,457 --> 00:04:25,609
or a cold beer.

131
00:04:25,789 --> 00:04:28,144
Maybe he just wanted ten minutes of

132
00:04:28,144 --> 00:04:30,096
quiet with the evening paper.

133
00:04:30,446 --> 00:04:31,200
Then dinner.

134
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:33,903
The whole family sat down together at

135
00:04:33,903 --> 00:04:34,585
the table.

136
00:04:34,765 --> 00:04:36,413
Dad sat at the head.

137
00:04:36,593 --> 00:04:39,159
Meat, potatoes and a vegetable.

138
00:04:39,339 --> 00:04:41,893
He might ask each child about school.

139
00:04:42,073 --> 00:04:43,664
Manners were expected.

140
00:04:43,844 --> 00:04:45,330
Elbows off the table.

141
00:04:45,510 --> 00:04:46,926
Napkin in your lap.

142
00:04:47,106 --> 00:04:49,810
And nobody left the table until they

143
00:04:49,810 --> 00:04:50,787
were excused.

144
00:04:51,137 --> 00:04:53,342
Discipline was often Dad's job.

145
00:04:53,522 --> 00:04:55,949
Wait until your father gets home was

146
00:04:55,949 --> 00:04:58,577
a phrase that struck fear into millions

147
00:04:58,577 --> 00:04:59,386
of children.

148
00:04:59,566 --> 00:05:03,005
Fathers in nineteen sixty were usually stricter

149
00:05:03,005 --> 00:05:04,395
than fathers today.

150
00:05:04,575 --> 00:05:05,945
Spankings were common.

151
00:05:06,125 --> 00:05:08,430
But many of those same fathers would

152
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:10,479
also do anything for their kids.

153
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:12,744
Dad also handled the money.

154
00:05:12,924 --> 00:05:15,048
He paid the bills at the kitchen

155
00:05:15,048 --> 00:05:16,310
table once a month,

156
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:18,567
writing checks and licking stamps.

157
00:05:18,747 --> 00:05:20,941
He balanced the checkbook with a pencil.

158
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:24,050
He gave Mom an allowance for groceries

159
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:25,823
and household expenses.

160
00:05:26,003 --> 00:05:28,255
And when a big purchase came up,

161
00:05:28,255 --> 00:05:29,945
like a new refrigerator,

162
00:05:29,945 --> 00:05:32,760
the family waited until Dad decided they

163
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,886
could afford it.

164
00:05:34,237 --> 00:05:37,371
After dinner, Dad might watch television with

165
00:05:37,371 --> 00:05:38,138
the family.

166
00:05:38,318 --> 00:05:40,607
Gunsmoke, Perry Mason,

167
00:05:40,607 --> 00:05:42,480
The Twilight Zone,

168
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,041
or a ball game.

169
00:05:44,221 --> 00:05:45,681
He controlled the channel,

170
00:05:45,681 --> 00:05:47,702
which meant getting up and turning a

171
00:05:47,702 --> 00:05:47,983
dial.

172
00:05:48,163 --> 00:05:50,303
He might fall asleep in his chair

173
00:05:50,303 --> 00:05:52,768
halfway through, and wake up just long

174
00:05:52,768 --> 00:05:54,908
enough to say he wasn't sleeping.

175
00:05:55,259 --> 00:05:57,659
Or he might be in the basement

176
00:05:57,659 --> 00:05:58,779
or the garage,

177
00:05:58,779 --> 00:06:00,460
working on a project.

178
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,103
Building a bookshelf.

179
00:06:02,283 --> 00:06:03,328
Fixing a lamp.

180
00:06:03,508 --> 00:06:04,959
Tinkering with the car.

181
00:06:05,139 --> 00:06:08,265
Many fathers of nineteen sixty could fix

182
00:06:08,265 --> 00:06:09,516
almost anything.

183
00:06:09,696 --> 00:06:12,581
They kept coffee cans full of screws

184
00:06:12,581 --> 00:06:14,665
and nails, sorted by size.

185
00:06:14,845 --> 00:06:17,200
Children learned the names of tools by

186
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:18,316
handing them over.

187
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:20,420
Some nights, he had a meeting.

188
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:22,299
The Elks, the Moose,

189
00:06:22,299 --> 00:06:23,573
the Lions Club,

190
00:06:23,573 --> 00:06:26,207
the American Legion or the VFW.

191
00:06:26,387 --> 00:06:27,490
The church council.

192
00:06:27,670 --> 00:06:28,704
The school board.

193
00:06:28,884 --> 00:06:31,043
The bowling league on Wednesday nights.

194
00:06:31,223 --> 00:06:34,601
Fathers in nineteen sixty belonged to things.

195
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:36,679
It was how they made friends and

196
00:06:36,679 --> 00:06:38,636
how they gave back to their town.

197
00:06:38,986 --> 00:06:41,598
Before bed, Dad made his rounds.

198
00:06:41,778 --> 00:06:43,788
He checked that the doors were locked,

199
00:06:43,788 --> 00:06:45,850
though many people never locked them at

200
00:06:45,850 --> 00:06:46,062
all.

201
00:06:46,242 --> 00:06:48,712
He turned down the thermostat to save

202
00:06:48,712 --> 00:06:50,047
on the heating bill,

203
00:06:50,047 --> 00:06:52,384
and turned off every light the kids

204
00:06:52,384 --> 00:06:53,185
had left on.

205
00:06:53,365 --> 00:06:54,354
Close the door,

206
00:06:54,354 --> 00:06:55,211
he would say,

207
00:06:55,211 --> 00:06:57,916
we're not heating the whole neighborhood.

208
00:06:58,266 --> 00:07:00,146
Saturday was for the house.

209
00:07:00,326 --> 00:07:02,824
Dad mowed the lawn with a push

210
00:07:02,824 --> 00:07:06,155
mower or a new gas-powered rotary mower.

211
00:07:06,335 --> 00:07:08,683
He washed the car in the driveway,

212
00:07:08,683 --> 00:07:10,410
with a bucket and a hose.

213
00:07:10,590 --> 00:07:12,071
He cleaned the gutters,

214
00:07:12,071 --> 00:07:13,230
painted the fence,

215
00:07:13,230 --> 00:07:15,806
and fixed whatever had broken during the

216
00:07:15,806 --> 00:07:16,128
week.

217
00:07:16,308 --> 00:07:18,343
The kids were expected to help,

218
00:07:18,343 --> 00:07:20,313
whether they wanted to or not.

219
00:07:20,663 --> 00:07:23,368
Saturday was also the day for errands.

220
00:07:23,548 --> 00:07:24,778
The hardware store,

221
00:07:24,778 --> 00:07:27,108
the barber shop and the gas station,

222
00:07:27,108 --> 00:07:29,309
where an attendant pumped the gas,

223
00:07:29,309 --> 00:07:32,093
cleaned the windshield and checked the oil.

224
00:07:32,273 --> 00:07:34,748
Many fathers took one of the kids

225
00:07:34,748 --> 00:07:35,198
along.

226
00:07:35,378 --> 00:07:36,955
Those Saturday errands,

227
00:07:36,955 --> 00:07:38,807
just the two of them in the

228
00:07:38,807 --> 00:07:41,001
front seat, are some of the most

229
00:07:41,001 --> 00:07:44,086
treasured memories people have of their dads.

230
00:07:44,436 --> 00:07:47,105
And Saturday afternoon might bring a ball

231
00:07:47,105 --> 00:07:47,431
game.

232
00:07:47,611 --> 00:07:50,486
Little League, where Dad was the coach

233
00:07:50,486 --> 00:07:52,301
or sat in the bleachers.

234
00:07:52,481 --> 00:07:54,537
Or a game on the radio or

235
00:07:54,537 --> 00:07:55,442
television.

236
00:07:55,622 --> 00:07:57,144
In nineteen sixty,

237
00:07:57,144 --> 00:07:59,511
baseball was America's game.

238
00:07:59,691 --> 00:08:02,066
Many fathers taught their sons,

239
00:08:02,066 --> 00:08:04,364
and sometimes their daughters,

240
00:08:04,364 --> 00:08:06,739
to throw, catch and keep score.

241
00:08:07,089 --> 00:08:09,795
Saturday night, Mom and Dad might go

242
00:08:09,795 --> 00:08:10,096
out.

243
00:08:10,276 --> 00:08:11,882
Dinner at a steakhouse,

244
00:08:11,882 --> 00:08:13,698
a movie, or a party at the

245
00:08:13,698 --> 00:08:14,885
neighbors' house.

246
00:08:15,065 --> 00:08:17,766
A babysitter came for fifty cents an

247
00:08:17,766 --> 00:08:18,141
hour.

248
00:08:18,321 --> 00:08:20,283
Dad put on a sport coat.

249
00:08:20,463 --> 00:08:22,021
Mom put on a dress and a

250
00:08:22,021 --> 00:08:22,994
little perfume.

251
00:08:23,174 --> 00:08:24,350
For a few hours,

252
00:08:24,350 --> 00:08:26,186
they were a couple again,

253
00:08:26,186 --> 00:08:27,435
not just parents.

254
00:08:27,786 --> 00:08:30,665
Every summer, there was the family vacation.

255
00:08:30,845 --> 00:08:33,197
Dad planned the route with a road

256
00:08:33,197 --> 00:08:34,979
map from the gas station,

257
00:08:34,979 --> 00:08:37,474
and packed the trunk like a puzzle.

258
00:08:37,654 --> 00:08:39,886
The family drove to the lake,

259
00:08:39,886 --> 00:08:43,273
the beach, the mountains or Grandma's house.

260
00:08:43,453 --> 00:08:45,148
Dad drove the whole way.

261
00:08:45,328 --> 00:08:48,427
He didn't stop unless he absolutely had

262
00:08:48,427 --> 00:08:50,968
to, and he always wanted to beat

263
00:08:50,968 --> 00:08:52,875
his time from last year.

264
00:08:53,225 --> 00:08:56,464
Sunday morning, the family went to church.

265
00:08:56,644 --> 00:08:58,594
Dad wore his best suit.

266
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:00,806
The kids squirmed in the pew.

267
00:09:00,986 --> 00:09:03,683
Afterward, he shook hands with the minister

268
00:09:03,683 --> 00:09:05,690
and talked with the other men on

269
00:09:05,690 --> 00:09:06,756
the church steps.

270
00:09:06,936 --> 00:09:08,759
Then it was home for Sunday dinner.

271
00:09:08,939 --> 00:09:11,110
Pot roast or fried chicken,

272
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:13,200
the best meal of the week.

273
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:16,220
Sunday afternoon was for relaxing.

274
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:17,923
Dad might take a nap,

275
00:09:17,923 --> 00:09:20,678
watch football, or take the family for

276
00:09:20,678 --> 00:09:22,853
a Sunday drive in the country,

277
00:09:22,853 --> 00:09:25,028
for no reason at all except to

278
00:09:25,028 --> 00:09:27,348
look at the farms and the trees.

279
00:09:27,528 --> 00:09:29,397
Kids sat in the back seat.

280
00:09:29,577 --> 00:09:31,806
Somebody always had to go to the

281
00:09:31,806 --> 00:09:32,433
bathroom.

282
00:09:32,784 --> 00:09:34,676
Or he might fire up the grill.

283
00:09:34,856 --> 00:09:36,128
By nineteen sixty,

284
00:09:36,128 --> 00:09:38,954
the backyard barbecue was a new suburban

285
00:09:38,954 --> 00:09:41,427
tradition, and it was Dad's domain.

286
00:09:41,607 --> 00:09:44,072
Hamburgers, hot dogs and steaks,

287
00:09:44,072 --> 00:09:46,999
cooked over charcoal with an apron and

288
00:09:46,999 --> 00:09:47,923
a long fork.

289
00:09:48,103 --> 00:09:49,612
Neighbors drifted over.

290
00:09:49,792 --> 00:09:52,103
The kids ran through the sprinkler.

291
00:09:52,283 --> 00:09:53,943
Those were good afternoons.

292
00:09:54,293 --> 00:09:55,371
And Sunday night,

293
00:09:55,371 --> 00:09:57,021
he got ready for the week.

294
00:09:57,201 --> 00:09:59,329
He polished his shoes on a sheet

295
00:09:59,329 --> 00:10:01,590
of newspaper on the kitchen floor.

296
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:03,279
He laid out a clean shirt.

297
00:10:03,459 --> 00:10:05,258
He wound the alarm clock.

298
00:10:05,438 --> 00:10:07,503
Then he looked in on the kids,

299
00:10:07,503 --> 00:10:10,257
already asleep, and went to bed himself.

300
00:10:10,437 --> 00:10:12,747
Six o'clock would come very soon.

301
00:10:13,097 --> 00:10:15,886
Most fathers of nineteen sixty didn't talk

302
00:10:15,886 --> 00:10:17,613
much about their feelings.

303
00:10:17,793 --> 00:10:19,772
They had grown up in the Depression,

304
00:10:19,772 --> 00:10:21,531
when there wasn't time for that.

305
00:10:21,711 --> 00:10:24,244
Many had seen terrible things in the

306
00:10:24,244 --> 00:10:26,495
war, and never spoke about them.

307
00:10:26,675 --> 00:10:29,312
Their children knew their fathers had been

308
00:10:29,312 --> 00:10:30,002
in the war,

309
00:10:30,002 --> 00:10:32,514
but often learned the details only after

310
00:10:32,514 --> 00:10:33,141
they died.

311
00:10:33,492 --> 00:10:35,372
They showed love differently.

312
00:10:35,552 --> 00:10:37,422
By working long hours.

313
00:10:37,602 --> 00:10:39,593
By teaching you to ride a bike,

314
00:10:39,593 --> 00:10:41,456
change a tire or bait a hook.

315
00:10:41,636 --> 00:10:44,258
By sitting through every school play and

316
00:10:44,258 --> 00:10:44,782
recital.

317
00:10:44,962 --> 00:10:47,149
By slipping you five dollars when you

318
00:10:47,149 --> 00:10:48,271
went off on a date.

319
00:10:48,451 --> 00:10:49,464
By being there,

320
00:10:49,464 --> 00:10:50,611
every single day.

321
00:10:50,961 --> 00:10:52,951
And many of them had a gentle

322
00:10:52,951 --> 00:10:55,286
side that only came out at certain

323
00:10:55,286 --> 00:10:55,697
times.

324
00:10:55,877 --> 00:10:58,291
Reading the Sunday funnies to a little

325
00:10:58,291 --> 00:10:59,244
one on his lap.

326
00:10:59,424 --> 00:11:01,352
Carving the Thanksgiving turkey.

327
00:11:01,532 --> 00:11:04,392
Putting together a bicycle at two in

328
00:11:04,392 --> 00:11:06,696
the morning on Christmas Eve,

329
00:11:06,696 --> 00:11:09,635
swearing quietly at the instructions.

330
00:11:09,815 --> 00:11:11,879
Walking a daughter down the aisle,

331
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:13,821
and trying very hard not to cry.

332
00:11:14,171 --> 00:11:16,648
Many children of those fathers say they

333
00:11:16,648 --> 00:11:19,569
didn't fully understand them until much later.

334
00:11:19,749 --> 00:11:22,094
When they had their own mortgages,

335
00:11:22,094 --> 00:11:24,440
their own jobs and their own kids.

336
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:28,119
Then, suddenly, they understood why Dad was

337
00:11:28,119 --> 00:11:31,130
so tired, why he worried about money,

338
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:33,246
and why he worked so hard.

339
00:11:33,426 --> 00:11:34,436
It was all for them.

340
00:11:34,786 --> 00:11:37,439
The role of fathers was starting to

341
00:11:37,439 --> 00:11:39,790
change, even in nineteen sixty.

342
00:11:39,970 --> 00:11:43,167
Television shows like Father Knows Best and

343
00:11:43,167 --> 00:11:46,141
The Donna Reed Show presented fathers as

344
00:11:46,141 --> 00:11:47,330
wise and gentle.

345
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:48,068
Dr.

346
00:11:48,248 --> 00:11:51,420
Spock encouraged fathers to be more involved

347
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:52,717
with their babies.

348
00:11:52,897 --> 00:11:56,336
Some fathers began changing diapers and pushing

349
00:11:56,336 --> 00:11:59,556
strollers, which their own fathers had never

350
00:11:59,556 --> 00:11:59,921
done.

351
00:12:00,272 --> 00:12:01,978
Over the next few decades,

352
00:12:01,978 --> 00:12:04,997
the role of fathers would change dramatically.

353
00:12:05,177 --> 00:12:07,926
Today's dads are often in the delivery

354
00:12:07,926 --> 00:12:10,531
room, at the pediatrician and in the

355
00:12:10,531 --> 00:12:11,110
kitchen.

356
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:13,920
But the fathers of nineteen sixty laid

357
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:14,958
the foundation.

358
00:12:15,138 --> 00:12:17,202
They gave their children stability,

359
00:12:17,202 --> 00:12:19,148
security and a chance at a better

360
00:12:19,148 --> 00:12:20,386
life than they'd had.

361
00:12:20,736 --> 00:12:22,599
If your father was a dad in

362
00:12:22,599 --> 00:12:25,222
nineteen sixty, take a moment to think

363
00:12:25,222 --> 00:12:25,705
of him.

364
00:12:25,885 --> 00:12:27,472
The hat on the hook by the

365
00:12:27,472 --> 00:12:27,777
door.

366
00:12:27,957 --> 00:12:30,198
The smell of his aftershave.

367
00:12:30,378 --> 00:12:32,535
The sound of his car pulling into

368
00:12:32,535 --> 00:12:33,385
the driveway.

369
00:12:33,565 --> 00:12:34,970
The way he said your name.

370
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:36,782
He may not have said much,

371
00:12:36,782 --> 00:12:38,476
but he said it every day by

372
00:12:38,476 --> 00:12:39,167
showing up.

373
00:12:39,517 --> 00:12:41,421
So tell us in the comments.

374
00:12:41,601 --> 00:12:43,545
What do you remember most about your

375
00:12:43,545 --> 00:12:43,923
father?

376
00:12:44,103 --> 00:12:45,716
What did he do for a living,

377
00:12:45,716 --> 00:12:47,214
and what did he teach you?

378
00:12:47,394 --> 00:12:48,851
We read every comment,

379
00:12:48,851 --> 00:12:50,970
and we'd love to hear about him.

380
00:12:51,150 --> 00:12:53,734
Next time, we're telling the story of

381
00:12:53,734 --> 00:12:56,038
the rise and fall of Woolworth's.

382
00:12:56,218 --> 00:12:58,043
Thank you for spending this time with

383
00:12:58,043 --> 00:12:58,191
me.
